    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "Chapters 1 and 2 both read " },
        {
          "text": "the open C chord",
          "link": { "kind": "article", "slug": "triad-open-chords-you-know" }
        },
        { "text": " on strings 5-4-3, " },
        { "text": "x 3 2 0 x x", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", as C major root position on this set. Move " },
        { "text": "4·2", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " back to " },
        { "text": "4·1", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and those same three strings are C minor root position." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "That's the whole minor move, sitting inside a chord shape your hand already knows without you having to learn a new one — which is exactly the kind of thing a learner tries at the first chance they get. Try it, and it's worth being precise about what actually changed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "warning",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "x 3 1 0 1 0", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is not a C minor chord. Strings 2 and 1 still sound " },
        { "text": "C4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "E4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " — the full six-string grip holds an " },
        { "text": "Eb", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and an " },
        { "text": "E", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " at once. The one-finger move only works if the other two strings are muted — lift them, or fret them somewhere other than "
        },
        { "text": "C4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "E4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", and only then is the chord under your fingers actually " },
        { "text": "Cm", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "This is the fullest register on the instrument, and chapter 2 already argued why — body resonance, plus how much of the comping vocabulary was built here. That argument doesn't change in minor. What's new is where the "
        },
        { "text": "b3", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " lands: " },
        { "text": "Cm", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " root position puts a minor third at the very bottom of the grip, " },
        { "text": "C3", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " to " },
        { "text": "Eb3", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ", where major put a major third. Major stacks its wider gap at the bottom of the grip and its narrower one on top; minor does the reverse. Up here that costs nothing — both ring perfectly clearly. The bottom of the grip is where the guitar's room runs out first, though, so it is worth holding as a question rather than an assumption: how low can a minor third sit before it stops being legible?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "One set left, and it's the one where that question about how low a minor third can sit stops being rhetorical: "
        },
        {
          "text": "the bottom set, where minor runs out first",
          "link": { "kind": "article", "slug": "triad-minor-strings-4-5-6" }
        },
        { "text": "." }
      ]
